From b349aadd6b2c1d4155915be703dd13ebfc72ab38 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ShorterKing <84120105+ShorterKing@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 09:36:47 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 14/15] econet: add support for Nokia G-140W-C

The Nokia G-140W-C is a GPON ONT with four gigabit LAN ports and
dual-band Wi-Fi, which ISPs in India such as ACT Fibernet and Tata Play
Fiber supply.

Hardware:
 - SoC: EcoNet EN7526G (EN751221 family), MIPS 34Kc, 2 threads, 900 MHz
 - RAM: 512 MiB DDR3 (Linux can use the first 0x1b800000 bytes)
 - Flash: 256 MiB SPI-NAND (Winbond W25M02GV) with the vendor BMT
 - Ethernet: 4x 1 GbE on ports 1-4 of the MT7530 module in the SoC,
   linked over TRGMII to the on-die switch
 - Wi-Fi 2.4 GHz: MT7592 (mt7603) on PCIe RC0
 - Wi-Fi 5 GHz: MT7612E (mt76x2) on PCIe RC1
 - USB: 2 ports (xHCI)
 - LEDs: power, PON, LOS, Internet, phone and USB on GPIOs; 2.4 GHz
   WLAN on the MT7592 LED pin
 - Buttons: reset (GPIO 0), LED on/off (GPIO 5)
 - Serial: 115200 8N1

Not supported: GPON and VoIP (closed vendor drivers).

The flash layout and bootloader match the Nokia G-240G-E. OpenWrt goes
into firmware slot A ("tclinux"): a 4 MiB TRX kernel followed by UBI.
Stock stays in slot B, and en75_chboot switches between them. LAN4 is
the WAN port.

Unlike on the G-240G-E, the bootloader loads its second stage (the
"bootram", with the bldr> prompt) from the slot it boots: an LZMA blob
after the kernel and rootfs in the slot's TRX, whose length is in the
TRX header. Without it the bootloader crashes and only falls back to
the other slot after nine failed boots. The bootram is Nokia's code, so
the image doesn't carry it: sysupgrade copies it from the router, from
slot A or else from the stock firmware in slot B, puts it at 0x3e0000
after the kernel and updates the TRX header to match. The image check
fails if no bootram can be found.

The bootloader also counts boots in the "flag" partition and switches
to the other slot when the count reaches nine. Stock clears it once it
has booted, and an init script does the same for OpenWrt.

The Wi-Fi calibration, a 512-byte EEPROM table per radio, exists only
as two files in the stock settings, on the UBIFS volume of the "config"
partition; the radios' efuses are nearly empty. sysupgrade copies the
tables into a static UBI volume, "calibration", next to the root
filesystem, and the device tree passes them to mt76 as NVMEM cells. To
read them it attaches the stock UBI, which is read-only in the device
tree, and mounts it read-only. The volume has to exist before
rootfs_data, which takes all the free space: if it is missing,
rootfs_data is removed first, since nand_do_upgrade recreates it.

Slot A has no BMT remapping. With it, a read error in old stock data
makes the BMT driver remap the block, and the bootloader redoes such a
remap on every boot once UBI has erased the replacement block, wiping
UBI data. Test images had remapped blocks 36-58 of the reference unit
that way, so UBI starts at block 59.

GRO is off on the switch ports by default. econet-eth has no checksum or
scatter-gather offload, so the CPU would split and checksum every merged
packet again before forwarding it: downloads through the device ran at
97 Mbit/s with GRO and 111 Mbit/s without.

Installation: boot the initramfs kernel through the bootloader's XMODEM
command and run sysupgrade -n with the sysupgrade image. Before writing,
it copies the bootram and the Wi-Fi calibration, and selects slot A with
en75_chboot. There is no
factory image, since slot A needs the bootram, which only the router has.

Tested on a G-140W-C: Wi-Fi on both bands, LAN1-LAN3 bridged and LAN4
as WAN with DHCP, the WAN LEDs, routing a 100 Mbit/s line at full speed,
booting from flash with settings kept across reboots and the boot count
cleared on each boot, sysupgrade of the installed system with settings
kept, switching to stock with en75_chboot factory and back, and a
sysupgrade from the RAM-booted initramfs that created the calibration
volume, after which both radios used the same calibration as with the
tables in the device tree.
